ion":"A full turn relative to the Sun takes about 24 hours; relative to distant stars, Earth rotates in about 23 hours and 56 minutes.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7005-962f-26ae54d35550","description":"~24 hours","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7973-961a-a05393bb7103","description":"~28 days","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-713a-a29e-59b182fd0e88","description":"~365 days","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-715a-8c3f-9178eeb6026e","description":"~1 hour","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-72ea-8922-96290f0869f2","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is biology the study of?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Biology\'s name comes from Greek roots meaning \u201clife\u201d and \u201cstudy.\u201d","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-793c-be4f-ff74dcd1596e","description":"Life","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-70f6-8b9c-f330a982baad","description":"Bell ringing","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7fdd-9803-f601058ac916","description":"Rheumatic diseases","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7f3b-8652-d5817fb9337f","description":"Syphilis","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-71cb-8b1b-577d583e4f0c","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Which scientist is said to have been inspired to develop his theory of gravity by a falling apple?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Isaac Newton\'s law of universal gravitation describes the same force pulling an apple toward Earth and keeping planets in orbit.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7df7-a5f4-a8c1567aa3fc","description":"Isaac Newton","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7130-93a1-4b41e16e9e5c","description":"Charles Darwin","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7a42-a2d2-367670fb8eb2","description":"Galileo Galilei","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7715-977a-0d620273aae7","description":"Ernest Rutherford","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7a01-80dc-53844709bc0c","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the word for a female fox?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"A vixen is a female fox; a male fox is often called a dog, and a young fox is a cub.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-742f-9db2-372970855115","description":"A Vixen","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-74a4-8065-475a899925fe","description":"A Ewe","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-77c3-9830-54dd8e7e4f27","description":"A Hen","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-71f0-832b-d68e545cad0b","description":"A Doe","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7161-8167-7a5bea5cf86f","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Siamese and Burmese are breeds of which animal?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Cats of the Siamese breed commonly have blue eyes and color-point coats, while Burmese cats typically have short, glossy coats.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-79d9-b41b-57405df22956","description":"Cat","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-704f-8761-4f3a30d57d16","description":"Dog","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-762b-96d1-f92263f633ed","description":"Donkey","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7dfc-a865-f784a8d4ab64","description":"Pig","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7754-acaf-925419be2297","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Who is known for his theory of evolution by natural selection?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Charles Darwin presented his theory in On the Origin of Species, drawing on observations from his voyage aboard HMS Beagle.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-762b-83d1-8a65233c788d","description":"Charles Darwin","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7bc1-988c-5641ed7fd5f3","description":"Albert Einstein","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-727d-b2c8-35f01de795a6","description":"Niels Bohr","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7932-8e30-dbf6da6418c8","description":"Sigmund Freud","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7b7e-8a2c-5a591a19592f","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"How many karats are in pure gold?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"24-karat gold is considered pure; lower ratings indicate that other metals make up part of the alloy.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-76f1-8929-e85d7edfa95d","description":"24 karat","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7812-b131-ff49034ea7a8","description":"22 karat","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7e53-965e-ef132e405cef","description":"30 karat","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7e71-9187-7f89c72e6e4d","description":"12 karat","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7c71-9a6d-69c4eab9cdf3","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is radar primarily used for?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Radar stands for \u201cradio detection and ranging\u201d; the time taken for reflected radio waves to return can reveal how far away something is.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7a37-8186-08c648598d7e","description":"Detecting objects","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7e31-9713-8992516e6e0b","description":"Amplifying sound","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-74e4-9db1-adb54bc76c9f","description":"Generating electricity","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7725-a55a-928f0112fbae","description":"Measuring temperature","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7342-a1af-67a4c084de42","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What instrument can we use to observe the stars?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"A telescope collects and focuses light, making distant stars easier to observe. Many large observatory telescopes use mirrors to gather that light.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7cde-a679-27d7b5af65df","description":"Telescope","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-70b5-a3aa-c28c0de3ea3e","description":"Periscope","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-797f-9d8d-a5c294df37ad","description":"Endoscope","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7f98-8465-f2ca755a67b8","description":"Microscope","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-78de-8fd7-52c8d0c1ac6e","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What theory describes the start of our universe?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"The Big Bang theory describes the universe expanding from an extremely hot, dense state. The cosmic microwave background is often described as the afterglow of that early universe.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7829-afa2-86320d38d066","description":"Big Bang","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-784b-8430-398adac219e4","description":"Supernova","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7292-bc87-737f26bbcbe0","description":"Standard model","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-75ea-834c-ff186c5aaecd","description":"Special relativity","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7b4d-afac-21318c8c346a","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the direct cause of Type I Diabetes?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"In Type I diabetes, the immune system destroys insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas, so blood glucose can build up.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7d8e-8462-ae10e74539e0","description":"Not enough insulin","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7991-b990-2eebfc327c2a","description":"Not enough estrogen","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7f42-82e1-fb4b4673c1c1","description":"Not enough calcium","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-78e1-b6e5-cb99c8e4ca91","description":"Not enough magnesium","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7fb8-a8cb-82de6bc4cce7","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the transition from liquid to solid phase?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Freezing is the reverse of melting; boiling and condensation are phase changes involving a gas.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7cc8-8af0-1d306d2e3f32","description":"Freezing","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-71d2-be71-6a50aea9cdde","description":"Melting","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7b40-a6e6-15431b125b3f","description":"Boiling","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-777f-8bb3-9452f242cf1a","description":"Condensation","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-73b0-b504-d88ec471abd4","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the name given to the practice of growing plants in liquids rather than soil?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Hydroponics supplies plant roots with nutrients through a water-based solution. The name combines Greek roots meaning \u201cwater\u201d and \u201clabor.\u201d","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7ff7-a14c-ecda06127c0f","description":"Hydroponics","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-73e1-b6fa-a20a903c546a","description":"Aquonics","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7708-8151-ad4ad4d891d4","description":"Horticulture","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-79e2-89bb-5eac90faed4a","description":"Waterculture","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7889-b7ac-1020875541a8","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is Earth\'s primary source of energy?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"The Sun supplies most of the energy reaching Earth, warming the planet and powering processes such as weather and photosynthesis.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7dbe-a1d8-5e7eefafee14","description":"Sun","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7a6f-b57e-63a166b4c810","description":"Moon","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3719-7f8d-9281-015f4acab9b1","description":"Venus","correc